  "read": {
    "headline": "Yields hit 2002 highs. Stocks slipped, oil rose 2%.",
    "standfirst": "Wednesday took back a little of Tuesday's records. The S&P 500 fell 0.22% to 7,801.77, the Dow 0.66% to 51,179.87 and the Nasdaq Composite 0.22% to 27,538.69, ending four- and five-day winning streaks, while the Russell 2000 fell 1.31% and industrials were the weakest sector at −2.18%. The ten-year touched 5.365%, its highest since 2002, before a $39 billion auction cleared at 5.300% with firm demand and the yield closed near 5.28%. The minutes said all 19 participants backed the September hike and most expect another by year-end, with no date. Gold fell to a two-month low, Bitcoin slid and European stocks lost about 1%, with Italy down 2.5% and French bonds selling off again. This morning Asia is lower, the Kospi down 1.4% and Singapore 3.1%, and Brent is $102.61, up 2.4%, on reports that Washington is weighing renewed strikes on Iran, Houthi attacks on Saudi Arabia and a Gulf storm. Today: claims, the thirty-year auction at 13:00 ET and Waller and Musalem.",
    "points": [
      {
        "title": "A small fall in the indexes. Industrials fell 2.2% and health care rose 1%.",
        "text": "The S&P 500 fell 0.22% to 7,801.77, the Dow 0.66% to 51,179.87 and the Nasdaq Composite 0.22% to 27,538.69, a day after records; the Dow and the S&P 500 ended four-session winning runs and the Nasdaq a five-day one. The Russell 2000 fell 1.31% to 2,793.20 and the VIX closed at 15.08. Health care rose 1.03% and was the only sector fund higher; industrials fell 2.18%, materials 1.51% and real estate 1.29%, while technology slipped 0.30% and is still up 7.20% over a month. Chip stocks led the weakness, with Microchip and NXP down more than 3%, and crypto-linked shares fell as Bitcoin dropped; the broker-dealer index fell 2.6%. Small caps, mid caps and recent IPOs all lagged the S&P 500 by 0.9% to 1.4%. Futures were mixed overnight."
      },
      {
        "title": "The ten-year touched 5.365%. The auction cleared at 5.300% and yields came back.",
        "text": "The ten-year yield touched 5.365%, the highest since 2002, and the thirty-year about 5.73%, during Wednesday's session, then eased after the $39 billion auction cleared at 5.300% with a bid-to-cover of 2.77 against 2.71 last time and a 2.52 average, and indirect bidders taking about 80% of the notes. The ten-year closed near 5.28%, up about 1bp, the thirty-year near 5.67% and the two-year near 4.77%. The long Treasury fund fell 0.17% and is down 6.15% over a month; municipals fell 0.49%, mortgage-backed 0.16% and emerging-market bonds 0.19%. The minutes of the September meeting showed all 19 participants supported the hike and most expect another by year-end; many said a higher path was prudent insurance against persistent inflation. October is priced as a hold at about 83%, with about three more quarter-point hikes priced by June, according to Morningstar. The New York Fed's survey showed one-year inflation expectations at their highest in more than three years."
      },
      {
        "title": "Brent is $102.61 after a report that the US may resume strikes on Iran.",
        "text": "December Brent is $102.61, up 2.41%, and WTI $90.20, up 2.17%, after both settled lower on Wednesday, Brent at $100.20 and WTI at $88.28, when the International Energy Agency agreed to speed the release of stocks and prioritise diesel. Overnight NBC reported that the White House and national security team have discussed restarting large-scale US military operations in Iran in the coming weeks, and The Atlantic reported that the Pentagon was asked for strike options before the November midterms. The Houthis' recent attacks on Saudi airports, Iran's attacks on tankers near Hormuz, and a tropical storm forecast to become a hurricane and reach the northern Gulf coast by the weekend add to it, against a surprise draw in US crude inventories and shipping through Hormuz briefly at pre-war highs. Heating oil is $4.71, up 1.14%, and natural gas is up 7.15% on the week. Brent's 5D column now lines up with WTI's, but its 1M column, +4.79% against WTI's −3.04%, still straddles the 30 September roll."
      },
      {
        "title": "Europe fell about 1%. Italy fell 2.5% and French bonds sold off again.",
        "text": "The Stoxx 600 fell about 1.1%, the DAX 1.4% to 25,091, the CAC 40 1.22% to 7,769.21, the FTSE 100 0.79% to 10,458.50, Italy's FTSE MIB 2.51% to 49,972 and Spain's Ibex 1.8%, with the bank sector down about 3.4% as French ten-year yields touched 4.92% and protests and the IMF's call for French fiscal tightening revived Tuesday's worries. The euro fell 0.63% to about $1.119 and is $1.12 this morning. German industrial production rose 2.0% in August, ahead of expectations, and the EU is reportedly weighing import restrictions on Chinese hybrid cars. The country funds fell 1.1% to 2.7% (Italy −2.74%, Netherlands −2.04%, Germany −1.40%, France −1.12%). Futures this morning point to a flat open in London and Frankfurt and a small gain in Paris. The ECB's accounts of its 10 September hike are out at 07:30 ET."
      },
      {
        "title": "Gold hit a two-month low. Bitcoin fell and the Kospi is down 1.4%.",
        "text": "Spot gold fell about 1.2% to near $4,114 on Wednesday, touching $4,066 for its lowest since 5 August, with the minutes and higher yields as the cited cause; futures settled at $4,140.70 and are $4,155.70 this morning, up 0.36%, but still down 6.38% over a month. Silver is $60.12, platinum up 1.66% and copper up 1.04%. Bitcoin fell more than 2% on Wednesday and is $82,834, down 0.53% this morning and 2.25% on the week, with Ether down 4.41% and Litecoin down 8.28% on the week. In Asia the Nikkei is down 0.84% near 69,446, the Kospi down 1.42% near 6,707 despite Samsung Electronics guiding to a record third-quarter operating profit of 107.4 trillion won, Hong Kong down 0.69%, Taiwan 0.84% and Singapore 3.13%; mainland China reopened after a week, with Shanghai down 0.27%. South Korean markets are shut on Friday for a holiday."
      },
      {
        "title": "Canada fell 2.1%, Brazil 1.5% and Chile 2.3%. The dollar was little changed.",
        "text": "The Canada ETF fell 2.10% and the TSX 1.70%, the Brazil ETF 1.47% and the Bovespa 0.74% to 204,302, and the Chile ETF 2.30%, in a broad Americas decline that matched the equity move. The Brazil ETF is still up 10.95% on the week after last Monday's election-driven surge. The dollar moved little: the euro is up 0.06% this morning, the yen down 0.14% at 158.27, the Canadian dollar down 0.07% and the Mexican peso 0.17% weaker, while the yuan strengthened 0.18% and the won 0.11%. The rupee is 96.74 per dollar after India's central bank hiked to 5.5% on Wednesday, 2.04% weaker over a month. Coffee fell 3.86% and cocoa 2.07%, while sugar, up 4.52% on the week and 15.08% over a month, held its gains. Lean hogs are down 17.98% over a month."
      }
    ]
